#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063826 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bd5c660-b216-59b4-b2bb-df83ef864b2a" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Concave Up and Concave Down Curves"}] :question/id #uuid "da0a95e3-c020-59c0-a3ca-4d809fea1224" :question/math-academy-id "e-10002" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029011/assets/f8377fe3d48db6f576ac6534d435b06baae510dd66c239782705c51f06fb7ec5.png)\n\nWhich of the curves above are concave up?" :question/worked-solution "The concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($\\smile$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($\\frown$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOnly graph II curves upward (like a \"smile\") among the given graphs."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063827 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a67f347a-30a5-5169-af2c-9a800be84e46" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Concavity and the Sign of the Derivative at a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "1869f910-361f-58c4-8076-5c4386d702f9" :question/math-academy-id "e-10003" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029011/assets/99a320e07046168bdd70d8168abcc6399de7e34044d6550a4d204a332110049b.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. For which of the following values of $x$ is $f(x)$ concave up *and* $f'(x) = 0?$" :question/worked-solution "The derivative $f'(x)$ is zero when $x$ is a stationary point (the tangent line is horizontal).\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that only $x=b$ and $x=d$ are stationary points.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029011/assets/d980345045691061434f86fb96138d0d4d20a38065e6606073974cd3b8f1d7bb.png)\n\nThe concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($\\smile$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($\\frown$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOf the points $x=b$ and $x=d,$ the graph is concave up ($\\smile$) at $x=b$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064037 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "51c15d7a-cd9c-58df-a750-97df0b695c64" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Intervals of Concavity From the Graph of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "6a860b08-3913-54d7-a3d6-ec15ce4553b4" :question/math-academy-id "e-6806" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029011/assets/7d62ee465505708dc91f486cf57953dc5058ad275fb5462b7217114d5c3e8ea0.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=f(x),$ defined on $[-3,3].$ Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave down." :question/worked-solution "The concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($\\smile$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($\\frown$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nWe can see that the function is curved downward like a frown ($\\color{black}{\\frown}$) over the intervals $(-3,-1)$ and $(1,3).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029011/assets/ad6e25c0611cda21ffcd41114afc4d601779edb2f31288ab16dd31207bf83f65.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292436 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "51c15d7a-cd9c-58df-a750-97df0b695c64" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Intervals of Concavity From the Graph of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77dbc399-6bf3-50e9-9bdd-373273c19b38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,1)$ and $(2,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292438} {:answer/id #uuid "196b9bcb-20c8-5a5d-af18-a948edab1c60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292439} {:answer/id #uuid "5de26cbc-848f-5091-852f-c7a1be6a9502" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292440} {:answer/id #uuid "33df19cb-fec5-50eb-8986-bb6d17d48089" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,1)$ and $(1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292441} {:answer/id #uuid "6e9c90ec-8525-5386-8079-4606f03fd782" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292442}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e9c90ec-8525-5386-8079-4606f03fd782" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292442} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b39882a-70c4-5be7-9f56-1ab000688635" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292437}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9e4201ba-1628-5c8d-9505-17e17eeae99d" :question/math-academy-id "q-114058" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029011/assets/2c9a731594d1c6a781543b5e5fad5b14e7905848ccf1d695cf2019bc931a8e78.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=f(x),$ defined on $[0,3].$ Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave up." :question/worked-solution "The concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nWe can see that the function is curved upwards like a smile ($⌣$) over the interval $(2,3).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029011/assets/ece77074a3d712b76ed55150910382ed5c0bb1ddf7d565bc2d2200a72394c4b1.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064418 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bd5c660-b216-59b4-b2bb-df83ef864b2a" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Concave Up and Concave Down Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fe04ab4-25c0-5c68-b1b6-924958447957" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061193} {:answer/id #uuid "296af852-54a2-51f7-8f2a-754218d7dfa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061194} {:answer/id #uuid "2d2d18e1-c2b0-55ac-9a13-ec6f70ee8a6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061195} {:answer/id #uuid "2d6659d5-1e86-5c8b-b40b-a40bdb8d2819" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061196} {:answer/id #uuid "3ed62ca1-2bd3-57f2-a97e-e97d21f3baae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061197}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d2d18e1-c2b0-55ac-9a13-ec6f70ee8a6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061195} :answer-field/id #uuid "31410164-585f-5ddd-a3b6-c37b6d40ae30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061192}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "362ddce8-046e-5791-a78c-7440cb5f1723" :question/math-academy-id "q-141508" :question/problem "![](/