he graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nWe can see that the function is curved upward like a smile ($⌣$) over the interval $(-1,1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029011/assets/b962ef8adde197bca30dfc5d0efe5bcbc24fef8c1fad374992e0c571ef7cd320.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292478 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "51c15d7a-cd9c-58df-a750-97df0b695c64" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Intervals of Concavity From the Graph of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f664c4a3-a0c6-5174-8db4-fa40f19467d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,1)$ and $(2,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292480} {:answer/id #uuid "3af4a446-ec6b-5072-9811-9266a04738b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,2)$ and $(2,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292481} {:answer/id #uuid "25ccc90e-04dc-5fb5-a8b3-2209e20bf2ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292482} {:answer/id #uuid "9841818d-94dd-5a84-b02b-66760a5e3240" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,3)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292483} {:answer/id #uuid "9c750dad-2cbe-5327-ae5f-ce0e27fc1659" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292484}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c750dad-2cbe-5327-ae5f-ce0e27fc1659" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292484} :answer-field/id #uuid "1df886d8-bcfb-5751-b607-d7354a93e678" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292479}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6547971b-4449-5718-b2be-9730e7b4edfd" :question/math-academy-id "q-141859" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029011/assets/f97651190b02f5e85baec69d8915840f76c0179689f7fbc05d70a91027325d88.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=f(x),$ defined on $[0,3].$ Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave down." :question/worked-solution "The concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nWe can see that the function is curved downward like a frown ($⌢$) over the interval $(1,2).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029011/assets/2ca2ab18c5a4945b197cfbf4e77e300ec73abed71ae7590063543742cbbf1322.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292485 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a67f347a-30a5-5169-af2c-9a800be84e46" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Concavity and the Sign of the Derivative at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18a9afea-33ad-5f86-b4aa-a3d1d85e5de8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292487} {:answer/id #uuid "4b1d8d6f-353a-5b94-bdb8-ed77947042ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292488} {:answer/id #uuid "d403c688-5019-5df4-b8ad-08d622b08a2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=d" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292489} {:answer/id #uuid "fc7f7d0d-d501-5608-8143-3332220b5609" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292490} {:answer/id #uuid "0aac7f92-59b7-500e-b7a0-d85ef671e132" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292491}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18a9afea-33ad-5f86-b4aa-a3d1d85e5de8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292487} :answer-field/id #uuid "39da3983-9428-5424-8cc6-8db82c82fd30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292486}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b071e3c4-7795-5d9a-a7ae-0a268a1729c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-142031" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029011/assets/47b3d021487b88f0a335fc070bfd4846a0ea54dd137f6a0db165db5b27180d45.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. For which of the following values of $x$ is $f(x)$ concave up *and* ${f}^{′}(x)=0?$" :question/worked-solution "The derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ is zero when $x$ is a stationary point (the tangent line is horizontal).\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that only $x=c$ and $x=e$ are stationary points.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029011/assets/899cb5979c943b1d385f9015ffa57bf97d4a47b10fb2be4e653272ed16494d73.png)\n\nThe concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOf the points $x=c$ and $x=e,$ the graph is concave up ($⌣$) at $x=c$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292492 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a67f347a-30a5-5169-af2c-9a800be84e46" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Concavity and the Sign of the Derivative at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ee5e8e6-9a48-514b-840f-b19cd2b54709" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292494} {:answer/id #uuid "6f1f551c-05cd-53a1-8b23-d4e1a52c0bbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292495} {:answer/id #uuid "241e11d9-6bf8-5eeb-b124-fd55a9a69563" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=d" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292496} {:answer/id #uuid "58ec77df-7714-53d1-8722-83ffc6b2bbf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292497} {:answer/id #uuid "a0a61ead-022e-543e-adff-08c37c425d5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292498}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58ec77df-7714-53d1-8722-83ffc6b2bbf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292497} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac3b336d-c69f-58e6-8e97-47efc7d31766" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292493}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f961413e-5822-575e-b3a8-c72a1388167f" :question/math-academy-id "q-142068" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029011/assets/d8d2a012db4de4a2d9a008bae9d7dae854eb6071e0c5da6d1e65eea6b93f8c97.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. For which of the following values of $x$ is $f(x)$ concave up *and* ${f}^{′}(x)>0?$" :question/worked-solution "The derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ is positive when $f(x)$ is increasing. From the given graph, we see that only $x=b$ and $x=c$ are on increasing parts of the graph.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029011/assets/be95fe97afc8c90a372176e3dcf19fe49493d0e92059e7ceb5a4a40e77f23ee6.png)\n\nThe concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOf the points $x=b$ and $x=c,$ the graph is concave up ($⌣$) at $x=b$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292499 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a67f347a-30a5-5169-af2c-9a800be84e46" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Concavity and the Sign of the Derivative at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f012a18a-629e-58f5-b979-8e88bb54813e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292501} {:answer/id #uuid "2779f16d-fe59-55b2-8d7d-849c0e06e0ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292502} {:answer/id #uuid "a2636fc7-ae38-5f6a-ac19-8881784bf911" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=d" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292503} {:answer/id #uuid "4369e77e-e37a-5c43-a2cb-c0a3bfe84d89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292504} {:answer/id #uuid "0493421a-9b42-542f-bcdf-70de7be6e81f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292505}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0493421a-9b42-542f-bcdf-70de7be6e81f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292505} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ef8d513-3e7b-5b29-86db-d1a6319aa486" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292500}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fe624ee0-07e2-5f60-b09e-05cadfdab83c" :question/math-academy-id "q-142069" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029011/assets/8dbc36f0b404c1704e9add4b668b257703d6d163f825695b9404a33633da3ea7.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. For which of the following values of $x$ is $f(x)$ concave down *and* ${f}^{′}(x)>0?$" :question/worked-solution "The derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ is positive when $f(x)$ is increasing. From the given graph, we see that only $x=a$ and $x=e$ are on increasing parts of the graph.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029011/assets/7f42ed4bdb45d14497c70b0e2e98af95442d9bf8e6aaa577766dec1c5df2b31f.png)\n\nThe concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOf the points $x=a$ and $x=e,$ the graph is concave  down ($⌢$) at $x=a$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292506 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a67f347a-30a5-5169-af2c-9a800be84e46" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Concavity and the Sign of the Derivative at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f21f1c05-1748-525f-b175-3f8acf07fcb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292508} {:answer/id #uuid "6c794db4-24af-5e40-b767-b364f464a0b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292509} {:answer/id #uuid "9e1e5296-f312-5ec1-a6a1-9d6f060d776d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=d" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292510} {:answer/id #uuid "6a7baf6f-7421-5806-b4c6-d6e77cdb170a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292511} {:answer/id #uuid "507f494c-a2d1-5c14-bb83-e6c003d96318" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292512}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f21f1c05-1748-525f-b175-3f8acf07fcb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292508} :answer-field/id #uuid "29314e2e-a371-549d-9638-7841d1df0675" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292507}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4d09fc32-948c-597f-bb8d-8a5ab5e45157" :question/math-academy-id "q-142070" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029011/assets/84e5e0eb9f97521c7c9fb57c79b5bda67a1747fd0e608f7b466f20acc78ca308.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. For which of the following values of $x$ is $f(x)$ concave up *and* ${f}^{′}(x)=0?$" :question/worked-solution "The derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ is zero when $x$ is a stationary point (the tangent line is horizontal).\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that only $x=b$, $x=c$ and $x=d$ are stationary points.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029011/assets/d5e3e8e54b3c3c7f055f82433c6f8a68ffbc4590230fb827a0b8713fdd808a40.png)\n\nThe concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOf the points $x=b$, $x=c$ and $x=d,$ the graph is concave up ($⌣$) at $x=c$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064758 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a67f347a-30a5-5169-af2c-9a800be84e46" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Concavity and the Sign of the Derivative at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84f68732-6230-5fb7-8b38-1f01d8fd5b6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063060} {:answer/id #uuid "8b6e9478-7454-5a8d-97b2-2e9de6d3eae2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063061} {:answer/id #uuid "a77c7a20-46db-5c91-abed-151eba2611d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063062} {:answer/id #uuid "029787d4-c10b-5916-8463-0995d6ff8910" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063063} {:answer/id #uuid "518a5b74-38ae-59fc-8256-705dfb6f649e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = d" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063064}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "029787d4-c10b-5916-8463-0995d6ff8910" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063063} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebda94a8-32bd-52b3-bfe9-fc59e00b79f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063059}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1da22a1d-290c-5139-8b45-bb9f57069390" :question/math-academy-id "q-48999" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029011/assets/2c735c35700b6cda3aeb608c97d36871eed46db48e9c77f9f118db6a8f09383a.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. For which of the following values of $x$ is $f(x)$ concave down *and* ${f}^{′}(x)<0?$" :question/worked-solution "The derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ is negative when $f(x)$ is decreasing. From the given graph, we see that only $x=a$ and $x=e$ are on decreasing parts of the graph.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029011/assets/8cf8b0b8ea68a7fcaac0456770dab60bf124721897e65a95701ec928730e388e.png)\n\nThe concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOf the points $x=a$ and $x=e,$ the graph is concave down ($⌢$) at $x=e$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064806 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "51c15d7a-cd9c-58df-a750-97df0b695c64" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Intervals of Concavity From the Graph of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9785058c-329b-55fc-9637-7ceee865761c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3, 1)$ and $(1, 3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063347} {:answer/id #uuid "aded52a6-93b0-511f-9f1b-d35eef943b7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3, 5)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063348} {:answer/id #uuid "e1d58dbe-fb8b-5092-b964-5ef385a425a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1, 3)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063349} {:answer/id #uuid "ec9fa222-9117-541a-9cf6-4897b1674212" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3, 1)$ and $(3, 5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063350} {:answer/id #uuid "57a776a1-07fd-5790-8507-49d58efecc56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1, 3)$ and $(1, 5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063351}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1d58dbe-fb8b-5092-b964-5ef385a425a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1, 3)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063349} :answer-field/id #uuid "2597a55f-1687-5449-83d5-f62940cb9a48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063346}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dd3f3945-1d74-5b64-9e26-f19b51b6c5b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-51250" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029011/assets/237c36808ded541ed264826866db3d2774dc2d5212f1292f21a6c25b3b07d0e1.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=f(x),$ defined on $[-3,5].$ Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave down." :question/worked-solution "The concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nWe can see that the function is curved downward like a frown ($⌢$) over the interval $(1,3).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029011/assets/a7d9436275984c12271f2c737eb005e1eaab5b308c2204040e7aff5e87faffb3.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064808 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "51c15d7a-cd9c-58df-a750-97df0b695c64" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Intervals of Concavity From the Graph of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8fdc57e-f0cb-54d3-866f-1fd4714b8f37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,-1)$ and $(1, 3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063359} {:answer/id #uuid "dfe80d7f-8f97-560c-b1e0-3ae6b74fd6bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,-1)$ and $(-1, 1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063360} {:answer/id #uuid "26dc4cac-0f76-5e69-adb7-b6626e58c263" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,-1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063361} {:answer/id #uuid "5dec0a34-2b2f-55a4-b953-7edf91491f93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1, 1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063362} {:answer/id #uuid "7fadbd8e-5049-5c49-a546-9e874d7366ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1, 3)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063363}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5dec0a34-2b2f-55a4-b953-7edf91491f93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1, 1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063362} :answer-field/id #uuid "055ef0c2-7de2-58f1-8dd4-e319395656cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063358}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dfdd409b-0b49-516b-81f3-51b6e085243a" :question/math-academy-id "q-51431" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029011/assets/2dbcf9be5f2a9ffe89bb0d8601e5be931619bbda9647ce5cf285365085183e74.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=f(x),$ defined on $[-2,3].$ Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave up." :question/worked-solution "The concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\".\n\nWe can see that the function is curved upward like a smile ($⌣$) over the interval $(-1,1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029011/assets/1c5e36abaafab3c3984edd11d99d40ded2b81c8c8e251678e6f617ca522da16c.png)"}]}
